From a06188d2057d3b7481f4eb0739006375fe3b4ea1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 08:52:51 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 24/25] WebGL: per-eye render targets for the camera hack

The camera hack drew a page's draws into the default framebuffer once per
eye, and its draws into its own framebuffers once, from the page's
camera. A scene drawn through a post-processing chain (three.js's
EffectComposer, bloom, Babylon's post-processes) is drawn into a page
framebuffer first and then copied onto the canvas, so both eyes showed
the same picture.

A page framebuffer now gets a right eye's copy of each attachment (a 2D
texture's level 0, a renderbuffer; same size, format and samples) when
the page draws its scene into it: a draw through the page's camera into a
framebuffer of the canvas's shape. Such draws are drawn once per eye, the
left into the page's framebuffer, the right into the copies. A draw that
samples a texture holding two eyes is drawn once per eye too, the right
eye's draw sampling the copies (with the texture's sampling state), and
so is every draw into a framebuffer that holds two eyes; clears and
blits follow, and a blit from such a framebuffer into the canvas gives
the canvas's right eye the right eye's picture. Copies start from what
the attachment holds. An upload into a texture leaves its copy behind
until the page draws into it again. Shadow maps (square, drawn from a
light) and other framebuffers stay one picture, drawn once.

The preference webgl.stereo-camera.render-targets (on) turns this off:
render targets then hold one view, drawn from the page's own camera, as
before.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code
